{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head a5a3406a82bfc2710415f69ef6b2479c9884a980, resting on all 54 artifacts, packages/room/README.md first.\n\nThis is useful phase-1 progress, not completion of lane A. P1.1 atomic redemption, P1.2 recovery-key revocation and P1.3 the final authority boundary are fixed in the inspected paths. P1.4 now re-runs require/carry and re-qualifies evidence against the new requirement using recorded admission facts. Duplicate approvals no longer produce a false met effect; same-entry attention pagination and the 100-item update boundary are fixed. Durable publication and workspace steps resume after interruption. The candidate correctly acknowledges P1.9 as unfinished. The following boundaries remain.\n\n1. P1, publication recovery identifies a pending commit by entry count, not exact commit identity (core.ts 1047). The exception to the foreign-writer fence accepts any opened publisher whose publishedThrough equals the pending through. Its publish of the already-present prefix returns that head after comparing entry lines. I forced lost push and read-back replies, then replaced the ref with a different commit containing identical entry lines but an invalid checkpoint signature; retry accepted the foreign SHA, sealed its checkpoint event, advanced publishedThrough, and deleted the pending cohort. The same occurs with a different parent. The stored pending.parent is not used to prove the resumed commit's identity. Persist or deterministically derive the exact expected commit from the original parent and owned cohort before remote I/O, and accept read-back only at the confirmed parent or that exact pending commit. Keep one implementation of Git serialization with the real lane L publisher. Add same-lines/different-checkpoint, retained-files and parent foreign-writer cases, including restart; keep successful lost-response recovery positive. This is an incomplete part of P1.5, not a request to abandon its durable-cohort design.\n\n2. P2, a workspace can become ready after its lease expires during fork creation (core.ts 875). The before/after predicate checks held state and lease generation but not expiresMs against the current clock. In the real SQLite/workerd alarm path I advanced the clock beyond the lease inside ensureFork; the workspace became ready under lease 1 while the held row's expiry was already past. The alarm's earlier leases step does not cover time elapsed during later I/O. Check the deadline before creating a fork and again before recording readiness, alongside the generation/state fence. Treat expiry as fenced and arrange the durable expiry/revocation work as appropriate. This reproduction does not claim a leaked workspace token; it shows a stale ready operation.\n\n3. P2, attention created later at the existing head falls behind issued live cursors. A live cursor records {as:head, an:MAX_SAFE_INTEGER}. The publication-error path adds the admin's publication-unresolved item at that same head (core.ts 1059) without a new entry. I took a live cursor, caused a foreign-writer publication error, and confirmed the warning exists in attention; poll with that cursor returns no entries or attention. Adding another entry still never delivers the warning. Do not add new attention behind an already-issued position. Use a monotonic attention position or a correctly sequenced causal event, and signal committed work so subscriptions wake. Preserve lossless pagination and cursor compatibility. This remains part of P2.8; the synchronous same-entry case is fixed.\n\n4. P2, a review that reopens an obligation omits the reopening from its sealed effects (admission.ts 967–975). The calculator returns opened and met, but review discards opened and emits an effect only if met is nonempty. I submitted Bob's approval, then Bob's objection on the same generation: the obligation changes from met to open in the read projection, while the objection's signed receipt contains effects:[]. Record both directions of the calculator's transition, emitting an obligations effect when either is nonempty. Keep the duplicate-approval no-transition case. This completes the proposed P2.7 consistency rule: the event should state the state change the projection reports.\n\n5. P2, existing durable storage needs an upgrade path (store.ts). CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS does not add attention.n or workspaces.attempts to tables made by the preceding revision. createSchema on a real SQLite fixture with those two preceding table definitions throws \"no such column: n\" while creating attention_position. Add an idempotent transactional schema migration before indexes and queries need the new columns; preserve old attention ordering/positions and pending workspace attempts. Review the legacy EvidenceRow admission fallback as part of the upgrade: an older self-review must not gain eligibility merely because missing author facts default to false. Test populated old storage and repeated reopen. No deployed Room outage is claimed; this is a reproduced storage-upgrade failure.\n\nP1.9 remains a completion condition, as the request and README state: wire the actual lane B engine and lane L publisher with the Artifacts adapter, test them jointly on shared SQLite, implement check carry and filtered checker inputs, and complete the Room's authority replay and the acceptance matrix. Adopt amendment 2's full lane A edits, including authorized repository onboarding and atomic canonical binding, transport/client changes, and sequenced recomputation/readiness decisions. Amendment 2 is approved and landed at main 5000a241; lanes B and L are still being revised. The matrix must eventually include Room-level integration for the push, reservation, recovery and lease races, even when another lane has unit coverage. Estimates are not a reason to drop any case.\n\nArchitecture: a serial admission queue, final synchronous boundary, one qualification/status calculator and named durable alarm steps form a clearer foundation. The remaining recovery fence must prove the exact remote commit, and attention needs an ordering model that remains valid when deferred work adds items. Production adapters and the new contract remain explicit unfinished work.\n\nSecurity: redemption now rolls back both acts and held secrets on refusal or failure, and revoked recovery candidates are denied. The exact-commit recovery gap can turn an unexpected writer into apparently confirmed publication. Deadline fencing must apply at the workspace's final boundary as it does at admission. The diagnostic work used local in-memory remotes and real workerd SQLite, not production access.\n\nSimplification: keep the single transition calculator and emit all of its changes. Keep one durable publication cohort and one Git implementation. A small versioned schema migration and one stable attention ordering rule are clearer than per-path exceptions. No second landing state machine should remain in the production path after P1.9.\n\nValidation: clean install; root workspace typecheck passed; unmodified Room suite 66/66 Node and 166/166 workerd, exit 0 (the runner emitted session-end/canceled-stream diagnostics). Six independent diagnostic tests reproduced the boundaries above, 6/6 workerd, in /tmp/artroom-checker-room-A-v2/packages/room/test/workerd/checker-v2-review.test.ts only. The first diagnostic run used an unsupported test operation ID; correcting it to op_ws_2_1 completed the expiry reproduction. Every candidate lockfile entry matches JSON from either the preceding reviewed Room lockfile or approved main 04e297b2: 327 entries, zero novel entries. All changed Room production and test files were inspected against the preceding review, with unchanged artifacts carried by exact content. No candidate source or builder worktree changed; no deployment or live Artifacts measurement was run.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4f6309932e67e0abeefeebd76a2dc49509041321",